===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ǵʰelh₃-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|gold}}, from {{inh|en|ang|gold|t=gold}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*golþ}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*gulþą|t=gold}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*ǵʰl̥h₃tóm|t=gold}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ǵʰelh₃-|t=yellow; gleam; to shine}}. Related to [[yellow]]; see there for more. Germanic cognates include {{cog|nl|goud}}, {{cog|de|Gold}}, {{cog|no|gull}}, {{cog|sv|guld}}, and cognates from other Indo-European languages include {{cog|lv|zelts}}, {{cog|ru|зо́лото}}, {{cog|fa|زرد|tr=zard||yellow, golden}}, {{cog|sa|हिरण्य}}. {{C|en|Gold}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~|gold|golds}}

# {{senseid|en|metal}} {{lb|en|uncountable}} A heavy yellow elemental [[metal]] of great value, with atomic number 79 and symbol [[Au]].
#* {{quote-book|en|title=A Further Range|author={{w|Robert Frost}}|chapter=The Vindictives|year=1936|passage=You like to hear about '''gold'''.<br>A king filled his prison room<br>As full as the room could hold<br>To the top of his reach on the wall<br>With every known shape of the stuff.<br>’Twas to buy himself off his doom.}}
# {{senseid|en|coinage}} {{lb|en|countable|_|or|_|uncountable}} A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so.
#: {{ux|en|The pirates were searching for '''gold'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|color}} {{lb|en|uncountable}} A deep yellow colour, resembling the metal gold.
#: {{color panel|gold|FFD700}}
#: {{color panel|metallic gold|D4AF37}}
# {{senseid|en|bullseye}} {{lb|en|countable}} The [[bullseye]] of an {{l|en|archery}} target.
#: {{ux|en|Daniel hit the '''gold''' to win the contest.}}
# {{senseid|en|gold medal as prize}}<!--id best not "medal" bc homoph w/ "metal"--> {{lb|en|countable}} A [[gold medal]].
#: {{ux|en|France has won three '''golds''' and five silvers.}}
# {{senseid|en|anything or anyone valuable}} {{lb|en|figuratively}} Anything or anyone that is very valuable.
#: {{syn|en|good as gold}}
#: {{ux|en|That food mixer you gave me is absolute '''gold''', mate!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Paul Hendy|title=Who Killed Simon Peters?|passage=Now obviously this meant that I went over my allotted time, but the theatre management didn't mind because I was giving them comedy '''gold''' and that's what gets bums on seats.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Victor Pemberton|title=Leo's Girl|passage=Marge Quincey didn't deserve a husband like his dad. He was pure '''gold''', and she wasn't worth a light beside him.}}
# {{senseid|en|teeth grill}} {{lb|en|slang|in the plural}} A [[grill]] {{gloss|jewellery worn on front teeth}} made of gold.

